!id: rcqfoccU3A
!front: State the \( \varepsilon \)-\( \delta \) definition of \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L \).
!back:
\( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L \) if for every \( \varepsilon>0 \) there exists \( \delta>0 \) such that for all \( x \) in the domain of \( f \),
\[ 0 < |x-c| < \delta \implies |f(x)-L| < \varepsilon. \]
!extra: ![The band of half-width epsilon around L and the punctured neighborhood of half-width delta around c (open circle at (c, L), since f(c) plays no role), showing the curve stays inside the band whenever x is within delta of c but not equal to c](img/epsilon-delta-limit.svg)
~~~

!id: jley1rlt49
!front: Does \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) \) depend on the value of \( f(c) \)?
!back:
No. The condition \( 0 < |x-c| < \delta \) explicitly excludes \( x=c \), so the limit depends only on the values of \( f(x) \) arbitrarily close to \( c \), not on the value at \( c \) itself.

Changing \( f(c) \), defining it when it was undefined, or deleting it when it was defined does not change \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) \), provided the function is unchanged at all nearby points \( x \ne c \).
~~~

!id: C6dkRqPvOl
!front: Why can a function have at most one finite limit as \( x \to c \)?
!back:
If \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=L \) and \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=M \), with \( L \ne M \), choose \( \varepsilon = |L-M|/3 \). For \( x \ne c \) sufficiently close to \( c \),
\[ |f(x)-L| < \varepsilon \qquad\text{and}\qquad |f(x)-M| < \varepsilon. \]
Then
\[ |L-M| \le |L-f(x)| + |f(x)-M| < 2|L-M|/3, \]
a contradiction. Hence \( L=M \).
~~~

!id: 8NFfUZShtQ
!front: Under what conditions do the algebraic limit laws hold for finite limits?
!back:
If \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=L \) and \( \lim_{x \to c} g(x)=M \), then:

- \( \lim_{x \to c} (af(x)+bg(x)) = aL+bM \) for constants \( a,b \)
- \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)g(x) = LM \)
- \( \lim_{x \to c} \dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \dfrac{L}{M} \), provided \( M \ne 0 \) and \( g(x) \ne 0 \) for \( x \) sufficiently close to \( c \), \( x \ne c \)
~~~

!id: ixauHpLMcn
!front: How do inequalities behave under finite limits?
!back:
If \( f(x) \le g(x) \) for all \( x \ne c \) sufficiently close to \( c \), and
\[ \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=L, \qquad \lim_{x \to c} g(x)=M, \]
then \( L \le M \).
!extra: A strict pointwise inequality \( f(x)<g(x) \) does not force \( L<M \). For example, \( 0 < x^2 \) for \( x \ne 0 \), but both sides have limit \( 0 \) as \( x \to 0 \).
~~~

!id: M4UiCaZPfr
!front: State the squeeze theorem for \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x) \).
!back:
If:

- \( g(x) \le f(x) \le h(x) \) for all \( x \ne c \) sufficiently close to \( c \)
- \( \lim_{x \to c} g(x)=L \)
- \( \lim_{x \to c} h(x)=L \)

then \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=L \).
~~~

!id: r1sHObTgCC
!front: When can a continuous function be moved outside a limit?
!back:
If \( \lim_{x \to c} f(x)=L \) and \( \varphi \) is continuous at \( L \), then
\[ \lim_{x \to c} \varphi(f(x)) = \varphi(L). \]
Equivalently, limits commute with applying a continuous outer function at the limiting value.
~~~
